{
  "abstract": "We prove a general upper bound on the number f of fixed vertices of a nonidentity automorphism of a directed strongly regular graph DSRG(v,k,t,lambda,mu) with t<k. A moved vertex has at most max(lambda,mu) fixed mutual neighbours; this bounds the number X of arcs between the fixed and moved sets, and a Cauchy-Schwarz count of 1_F'A^2 1_F with two slack terms gives an inequality that excludes f when no admissible X satisfies it (Theorem A). For prime order p>k-t the bound sharpens (Theorem A'). The bound is tight on SRG(45,12,3,3) at f=15, and a control on 1,886 stored DSRGs (95,091 automorphisms) found no violation. Combining it with the trace split of De Winter-Kamischke-Wang (Lemma B, their Theorem 2.3), hand arguments for p>mu, and 16 SAT instances whose UNSAT answers are checked by DRAT, we restrict the prime-order automorphisms of the two open sets DSRG(54,15,13,4,4) and DSRG(63,18,14,5,5): for 54 only p in {2,3} occur (so |Aut| = 2^a 3^b), with f in {0,15,18,21,24} for p=3 and f even, f<=28, for p=2; for 63 only p in {2,3,5,7}, order 7 is fixed-point-free with s=3, and f is restricted for p=2,3,5. The same holds for the complements. No parameter set is settled: graphs with trivial or small automorphism groups are untouched.",
